(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug approached with caution in older adults with pericarditis?","choices":["It appears on the Beers list","It prolongs the QT interval","It calcifies the pericardium","It raises the risk of tamponade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In older adults, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs appear on the Beers list of potentially inappropriate medications and call for caution; colchicine may need a dose reduction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which description fits a pericardial friction rub?","choices":["Scratchy, triphasic, loudest at the left sternal border","Low-pitched diastolic rumble heard best at the apex","Blowing holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla","Basal crackles that clear after the patient coughs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The rub is classically triphasic with a superficial scratchy or squeaking quality, waxes and wanes, is loudest over the left sternal border and is best heard with the diaphragm. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Besides idiopathic disease, which etiology is singled out as especially important behind pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Hypertension","Atrial fibrillation","Malignancy","Coxsackievirus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neoplastic disease is an important underlying etiology, especially for pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade, so a large effusion should raise suspicion for cancer. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is the most common disorder involving the pericardium?","choices":["Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Acute pericarditis","Pericardial effusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial disease presents as acute or recurrent pericarditis, effusion without major compromise, or a compressive syndrome, and acute pericarditis is the most common pericardial disorder. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which condition is in the differential diagnosis of acute cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Congestive heart failure","Massive pulmonary embolism","Liver cirrhosis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute tamponade is mimicked by myocardial infarction (especially with right ventricular involvement), aortic dissection and massive pulmonary embolism with right ventricular overload. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which additional measure accompanies anti-inflammatory therapy for pericarditis?","choices":["Prophylactic antibiotics","A loop diuretic for all","Gastrointestinal protection","Routine anticoagulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal protection, such as a proton pump inhibitor, is provided because prolonged anti-inflammatory therapy strips the stomach of its protective prostaglandins. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"During activity restriction for acute pericarditis, the maximal heart rate should stay below what?","choices":["100 beats per minute","60 beats per minute","140 beats per minute","180 beats per minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Activity is limited to keep the maximal heart rate under 100 beats per minute for at least 1 month and until symptoms resolve and C-reactive protein normalizes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which three findings make up the Beck triad?","choices":["Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respirations","Pulsus paradoxus, electrical alternans, Kussmaul sign","Fever, a new murmur and splinter hemorrhages in nails","Hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Beck triad of cardiac tamponade is distant (muffled) heart sounds, distended jugular veins and decreased arterial blood pressure, though the complete triad is present in only a minority. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Pericarditis that develops after a myocardial infarction or cardiac surgery carries which eponym?","choices":["Beck triad","Kussmaul sign","Spodick sign","Dressler syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis after myocardial infarction or after cardiotomy (cardiac surgery) is Dressler syndrome, typically appearing a few weeks after the injury as the damaged tissue inflames the pericardium. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"In acute pericarditis, which change appears in lead aVR?","choices":["ST elevation and PR depression","Deep Q waves and inverted T waves","A delta wave with a short PR","ST depression and PR elevation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lead aVR (and sometimes V1) shows the reciprocal pattern, ST depression and PR elevation, while most other leads show ST elevation and PR depression. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is electrical alternans?","choices":["PR interval lengthening until a beat drops","ST elevation that shifts between leads","An irregular rhythm without P waves","QRS amplitude alternating beat to beat"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Electrical alternans is an alternating amplitude of the QRS complex from beat to beat, as the heart swings within a large effusion; it is fairly specific for tamponade. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Population data suggest a pericardial effusion may be present in up to what share of the general adult population?","choices":["40%","6.5%","0.5%","25%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Framingham cohort data suggest a pericardial effusion may be present in up to 6.5% of the general adult population, often small and unnoticed by the patient. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why can a chronic pericardial effusion grow large without causing tamponade?","choices":["Chronic effusions are always loculated","The pericardium stretches over time","Chronic fluid is never under pressure","The ventricles hypertrophy to resist it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic effusions allow a large amount of fluid to accumulate over time without tamponade because the pericardium stretches; acute effusions can compromise the heart with small amounts. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which anti-inflammatory drug is used for pericarditis that follows a myocardial infarction?","choices":["Indomethacin","Prednisone","Aspirin","Ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs are not given for post-infarction pericarditis; aspirin is used instead, continued from the infarction care, and colchicine is added. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Pericarditis symptoms that persist beyond 4 to 6 weeks, up to 3 months, without a sustained remission define which form?","choices":["Recurrent pericarditis","Chronic pericarditis","Constrictive pericarditis","Incessant pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Incessant pericarditis is when symptoms persist for more than 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission; past 3 months the label becomes chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram pattern is characteristic of acute pericarditis?","choices":["Low voltage with QRS amplitude alternating","Peaked T waves with a widening QRS complex","Widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression","Convex ST elevation in one coronary territory"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Characteristic changes are widespread concave ST elevation and PR depression across most limb leads and V2-V6, with reciprocal ST depression and PR elevation in aVR. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"When is prednisone used in place of a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug for acute pericarditis?","choices":["For every patient with a friction rub","When those drugs are contraindicated","Whenever fever rises above 38 degrees","Only after anti-interleukin-1 therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prednisone, with colchicine, is second line: for cases refractory to first-line therapy or when a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug cannot be taken. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In an older adult with pericardial effusion or tamponade, which etiology warrants a higher index of suspicion?","choices":["Malignancy","Coxsackievirus","Rheumatic fever","Kawasaki disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In elderly patients there should be a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade, whereas younger patients are more often idiopathic or viral. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"After a first recurrence of pericarditis, colchicine is typically continued for how long?","choices":["About 3 months","About 2 weeks","Lifelong therapy","6 months or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A first recurrence is treated with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug until pain resolves and inflammatory markers normalize, plus colchicine for 6 months or more. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the definitive treatment for established constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Lifelong colchicine","Cardiac transplantation","Pericardiectomy","Pericardiocentesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis is often a surgical disease; pericardiectomy, surgical removal of part or most of the pericardium, is the definitive treatment and improves survival. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which noncardiac condition belongs in the differential of constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Pneumonia","Cirrhosis","Asthma","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis belongs in the differential because both produce ascites and fluid overload; subacute tamponade and restrictive cardiomyopathy complete the list. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"At a second recurrence, low-to-moderate dose prednisone is reserved for which patients?","choices":["Those with systemic autoimmune disease","Those with a typical viral prodrome","Those with idiopathic disease","Those with post-surgical injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At a second recurrence, an interleukin-1 blocker is added for idiopathic or post-cardiac injury disease that is colchicine-resistant with prior C-reactive protein elevation, while prednisone is for underlying systemic autoimmune disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which test is required to confirm a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Electrocardiogram","Cardiac catheterization","Echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical evaluation, electrocardiogram and chest radiograph may suggest an effusion, but an echocardiogram is required to confirm it; it is specific and sensitive for detecting fluid. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"In the symptom-based approach, tapering starts once the patient has been symptom-free for at least how long?","choices":["24 hours","1 hour","7 days","4 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When duration is based on symptom resolution alone, tapering starts once the patient has been symptom-free for at least 24 hours; the alternative adds C-reactive protein normalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In suspected constriction, what does a chest radiograph without pericardial calcification mean?","choices":["It confirms restrictive cardiomyopathy","It means tamponade is the cause","It does not exclude the diagnosis","It rules out constrictive disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcification is highly consistent with constriction when present, but the majority of patients do not have it on chest radiograph, so its absence does not rule the diagnosis out. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which etiology of constrictive pericarditis carries the worst survival?","choices":["Post-cardiac surgery","Prior ionizing radiation","Idiopathic disease","Viral infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Etiology is an important determinant of survival, and prior ionizing radiation is associated with the worst survival rates; its damage is very hard to reverse. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph appearance suggests a large pericardial effusion?","choices":["Bat-wing perihilar opacities","Globular, water-bottle cardiac silhouette","Boot-shaped heart with an upturned apex","Egg-on-a-string cardiac silhouette"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A large effusion enlarges the cardiac silhouette into a globular water-bottle shape, often with clear lung fields; at least 200 mL must accumulate before the silhouette enlarges. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How often is the complete Beck triad present in cardiac tamponade?","choices":["In only a minority of cases","In nearly every case","In about half of cases","Only in chronic effusions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complete classic Beck triad (hypotension, dilated jugular veins, muffled heart sounds) is present in only a minority of cases, so its absence does not exclude tamponade. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition is a key mimic in the differential of constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Isolated aortic stenosis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Acute myocardial infarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The differential of constrictive pericarditis is subacute cardiac tamponade, restrictive cardiomyopathy and cirrhosis; natriuretic peptide is likely lower in constriction than in restrictive disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which bacterial cause of pericarditis is linked to rheumatic pancarditis?","choices":["Streptococcus","Staphylococcus","Mycoplasma","Histoplasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Among the bacterial causes, Streptococcus is tied to rheumatic pancarditis; the others listed are Staphylococcus, Pneumococcus, Haemophilus, Mycoplasma and Mycobacterium tuberculosis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"}]};
